text:
The sample space \Omega. all possible outcomes of an experiment.
Example: for a coin toss, \Omega = \{\text{Heads}, \text{Tails}\}.

Events subsets of the sample space. We use capital letters A, B, \ldots to denote events.

The complement of A is A^c, meaning A does not occur.

The intersection A \cap B means A and B occur.

The union A \cup B means A or B (or both) occur.


A probability distribution \mathrm{Pr}. Assigns numbers between 0 and 1 to events, with \mathrm{Pr}(\Omega)=1.









Definition 1.1 (Complement, Intersection and Union)  

Complement: A^c = \{x \in \Omega \mid x \notin A \}.
Intersection: A\cap B = \{x \in \Omega \mid x \in A \text{ and } x \in B\}.

Union: A\cup B = \{x \in \Omega \mid x \in A \text{ or } x \in B\}.








Show Visualisation

// Toggle: highlight selection (use explicit values for robust comparisons)
viewof vennOp = Inputs.radio(
  ["None", "A", "B", "Aᶜ", "Union", "Intersection"],
  { value: "None", label: "Highlight:" }
)







venn = {
  const width = 480, height = 280;
  const pad = 18;

  const cxA = 190, cyA = 140, r = 90;   // circle A
  const cxB = 290, cyB = 140;           // circle B

  const svg = d3.create("svg")
    .attr("width", width)
    .attr("height", height)
    .attr("viewBox", [0, 0, width, height]);

  // Sample space Ω (bounding rectangle)
  const omega = svg.append("rect")
    .attr("x", pad).attr("y", pad)
    .attr("width", width - 2*pad).attr("height", height - 2*pad)
    .attr("rx", 10)
    .attr("fill", "none")
    .attr("stroke", "currentColor")
    .attr("stroke-opacity", 0.5);

  // Label Ω
  svg.append("text")
    .attr("x", pad + 8).attr("y", pad + 16)
    .text("Ω")
    .style("font-weight", 400)
    .style("font-size", "18px")
    .style("fill", "var(--fg-muted)");

  // Colors (CSS vars with fallbacks)
  const colA   = "var(--brand-teal, #14b8a6)";
  const colB   = "var(--brand-red, #ef4444)";
  const hilite = "var(--brand-orange, #F39C65)";
  const grid   = "var(--plot-grid, #94a3b8)";

  // --- Defs: clips & mask for A^c ---
  const defs = svg.append("defs");

  defs.append("clipPath").attr("id", "clipA")
    .append("circle").attr("cx", cxA).attr("cy", cyA).attr("r", r);

  defs.append("clipPath").attr("id", "clipB")
    .append("circle").attr("cx", cxB).attr("cy", cyB).attr("r", r);

  // Mask for A^c: white keeps, black removes (subtract circle A from Ω rect)
  const m = defs.append("mask").attr("id", "maskAc");
  m.append("rect")
    .attr("x", pad).attr("y", pad)
    .attr("width", width - 2*pad).attr("height", height - 2*pad)
    .attr("rx", 10)
    .attr("fill", "white");
  m.append("circle")
    .attr("cx", cxA).attr("cy", cyA).attr("r", r)
    .attr("fill", "black");

  // Base sets (always lightly transparent)
  svg.append("circle")
    .attr("cx", cxA).attr("cy", cyA).attr("r", r)
    .attr("fill", colA).attr("fill-opacity", 0.4);

  svg.append("circle")
    .attr("cx", cxB).attr("cy", cyB).attr("r", r)
    .attr("fill", colB).attr("fill-opacity", 0.4);

  // Highlight layer
  if (vennOp === "A") {
    svg.append("circle")
      .attr("cx", cxA).attr("cy", cyA).attr("r", r)
      .attr("fill", hilite).attr("fill-opacity", 0.99);

  } else if (vennOp === "B") {
    svg.append("circle")
      .attr("cx", cxB).attr("cy", cyB).attr("r", r)
      .attr("fill", hilite).attr("fill-opacity", 0.99);

  } else if (vennOp === "Aᶜ") {
    // Draw Ω in orange, but masked to remove A
    svg.append("rect")
      .attr("x", pad).attr("y", pad)
      .attr("width", width - 2*pad).attr("height", height - 2*pad)
      .attr("rx", 10)
      .attr("fill", hilite).attr("fill-opacity", 0.99)
      .attr("mask", "url(#maskAc)");

  } else if (vennOp === "Union") {
    // A ∪ B: overlay both circles solid orange
    svg.append("circle")
      .attr("cx", cxA).attr("cy", cyA).attr("r", r)
      .attr("fill", hilite).attr("fill-opacity", 0.99);
    svg.append("circle")
      .attr("cx", cxB).attr("cy", cyB).attr("r", r)
      .attr("fill", hilite).attr("fill-opacity", 0.99);

  } else if (vennOp === "Intersection") {
    // A ∩ B: clip B by A (equivalently clip A by B)
    svg.append("g").attr("clip-path", "url(#clipA)")
      .append("circle")
      .attr("cx", cxB).attr("cy", cyB).attr("r", r)
      .attr("fill", hilite).attr("fill-opacity", 0.99);
  }

  // Labels A and B
  svg.append("text")
    .attr("x", cxA - 26).attr("y", cyA - 8)
    .text("A")
    .style("font-size", "14px")
    .style("font-weight", 600)
    .style("fill", "var(--fg-muted)");

  svg.append("text")
    .attr("x", cxB + 22).attr("y", cyB - 8)
    .text("B")
    .style("font-size", "14px")
    .style("font-weight", 600)
    .style("fill", "var(--fg-muted)");

  // Subtle baseline
  svg.append("line")
    .attr("x1", pad).attr("x2", width - pad)
    .attr("y1", height - pad - 0.5).attr("y2", height - pad - 0.5)
    .attr("stroke", grid).attr("stroke-opacity", 0.2);

  return svg.node();
}








Figure 1.1: Venn diagram of two events A and B inside the sample space \Omega.













Definition 1.2 (Addition rule (inclusion–exclusion)) For any two events A, B\subseteq \Omega, we have 
\mathrm{Pr}(A \cup B) = \mathrm{Pr}(A) + \mathrm{Pr}(B) - \mathrm{Pr}(A \cap B).












Example 1.1 (A quick inclusion–exclusion check) Roll a fair six-sided die. Let A=\{\text{even}\} and B=\{\text{number} \ge 4\}. Compute \mathrm{Pr}(A\cup B).

   Solution 
We have

\mathrm{Pr}(A)=3/6=1/2 (even outcomes 2,4,6).

\mathrm{Pr}(B)=3/6=1/2 (outcomes 4,5,6).

\mathrm{Pr}(A\cap B)=2/6=1/3 (outcomes 4,6).

By inclusion–exclusion 1.2, 
\mathrm{Pr}(A\cup B)=\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}-\tfrac{1}{3}=\tfrac{2}{3}.













Definition 1.3 (Mutually Exclusive, Exhaustive Events, and Partitions)  

Two events A and B are mutually exclusive (or disjoint) if they cannot occur together: 
\mathrm{Pr}(A \cap B) = 0.

Two events A and B are exhaustive if at least one of them must occur: 
\mathrm{Pr}(A \cup B) = 1.

More generally, a collection of events \{B_1,\ldots,B_k\} is exhaustive if \bigcup_{i=1}^k B_i = \Omega, where \Omega is the sample space.
A collection of events \{B_1,\ldots,B_k\} forms a partition of \Omega if they are both mutually exclusive and exhaustive. Equivalently,

B_i \cap B_j = \varnothing for all i \neq j (non-overlapping), and

\bigcup_{i=1}^k B_i = \Omega (their union is the whole sample space).








